{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-82320",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's refer to our standard ${30}^{∘}$-${60}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14165760/assets/85f119cdc6b7a80dd7cd4ea2a2a235e80723b7c2fe6837fa127beb8fd994d7ea.png)\n\nSince this is a ${30}^{∘}$-${60}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle, the length of the longer leg is $\\sqrt{3}$ times that of the shorter leg.\n\nHere, the shorter leg has length $x=2\\sqrt{3}$ and the longer leg has length $\\sqrt{3}x,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC=\\sqrt{3}x=\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}=3\\cdot 2=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the length of the longer leg is $6.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
